<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bago</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pegu, Bago, Myanmar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bago, formerly Pegu, is a city in southern Myanmar, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>. It was the capital of the Mon kingdom of Hanthawaddy from 1369, of the Toungoo kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> in the 16th century, when it was one of the great cities of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and of the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom from 1740 to 1757. It is known for the Shwemawdaw Pagoda and the huge reclining Buddha of Shwethalyaung.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bago.jpg" alt="Bago" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christopher Voitus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bago-Rundblick_von_Mahazedi_Paya_(4).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
